|> Exercise
    handle=_where_is_the_pythagorean_identity_
    Where or how, if at all, is the
    __pythagorean identity__
    apparent in the four graphs of Exercise >>_four_graphs_?

    |> Solution
        !! Imagine wooden cutouts of $y = \sin^2(x)$, $y = \cos^2(x)$:
        Imagine turning the graphs $y = \sin^2(x)$
        and $y = \cos^2(x)$ into wooden cutouts:

        |> Image
            style=margin-top:1.1em;margin-bottom:1.1em;
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_pythagorean_graphs_sin2.svg
        |> Image
            style=margin-top:1.1em;margin-bottom:1.1em;
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_pythagorean_graphs_cos2.svg

        It seems that if we would vertically flip
        one of the cutouts (in this case $y = \cos^2(x)$),
        it would perfectly fit on top of the other to make the
        constant function $y = 1$:

        |> Image
            style=margin-top:1.1em;margin-bottom:1.1em;
            src=images/svg_ch5_ex_pythagorean_graphs_compounded.svg

        !! This is not only appearances, but is indeed the case: 
        This is no illusion: 
        it occurs because 
        !! it occurs because 
        !! of the pythagorean identity, as
        !! the latter states that 
        $$
        \cos^2(x) + \sin^2(x) = 1
        $$
        for all $x \in \rr$, 
        !! which is exactly what is
        !! required for the two functions to stack up to
        !! $y = 1$!
        !! which is
        i.e., because of 
        the pythagorean identity!
        !! is required for the two functions two stack up to
        !! $y = 1$!
        !! i.e., because !
